    "",
    "WHY IT EXISTS (owner, 2026-08-16, asking whether audits should be re-run periodically):",
    "a ✅ is a statement about code that existed ON THAT DATE, and the table says so in prose —",
    "but nothing acted on it. Row 7 audited the dashboard on 08-09; its whole loading model was",
    "replaced on 08-11; the row still read ✅ while five bugs came out of exactly that change.",
    "",
    "Re-running every audit on a schedule is the answer that never finishes and mostly re-reads",
    "code nobody touched. The answer that does finish is: re-open a row when the code UNDER it",
    "has moved since the day it was marked. `npm run audit:drift` asks git that question.",
    "",
    "`paths` is what the row is ABOUT — a handful of entries, not an exhaustive file list. Too",
    "wide and every row is permanently stale (src/); too narrow and the change that matters slips",
    "past. When an audit is done, write the paths the audit actually read.",
